WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.119
Engineering and configuration hunts focus on privileged activity that can alter control-system behavior.

00:00:07.119 --> 00:00:18.165
Relevant evidence may come from engineering workstations, controller logs, project repositories, remote sessions, file systems, removable-media records, and formal change management.

00:00:18.165 --> 00:00:20.865
Start with approved work.

00:00:20.865 --> 00:00:28.475
Identify maintenance windows, assigned engineers, target assets, expected project versions, tools, and change tickets.

00:00:28.475 --> 00:00:36.576
Then compare observed connections, files, controller mode changes, downloads, and configuration exports with that authorization.

00:00:36.576 --> 00:00:45.167
Controller logic or parameter changes deserve careful handling because legitimate updates are common and operationally significant.

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:50.322
Firmware work may also change network behavior or logging.

00:00:50.322 --> 00:01:00.387
Hunters should verify vendor guidance, project records, backups, and engineering approval rather than judging a change from one event.

00:01:00.387 --> 00:01:08.978
Unauthorized project-file movement can include copies to unapproved systems, removable media, shared folders, or remote sessions.

00:01:08.978 --> 00:01:14.133
The concern may be integrity, confidentiality, or uncontrolled versioning.

00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:22.725
Preserve file metadata and repository history where available without opening or altering production project files unnecessarily.

00:01:22.725 --> 00:01:28.862
Distinguish suspicious activity from troubleshooting, emergency repair, commissioning, and routine maintenance.

00:01:28.862 --> 00:01:36.471
Operators and control engineers can confirm whether the process state and observed work align.

00:01:36.471 --> 00:01:42.117
Security teams can correlate identity, endpoint, network, and change evidence.

00:01:42.117 --> 00:01:49.236
Document the exact artifact, source, destination, account, time, approved version, and process impact.

00:01:49.236 --> 00:01:55.373
If escalation is required, preserve known-good backups and coordinate response authority.

00:01:55.373 --> 00:02:01.510
Unplanned rollback, isolation, or controller reload can create serious operational risk.
